⭐ 1. How do you work with data scientists to convert research notebooks into production-ready code?
Answer (5 yrs experienced level):

Converting notebooks to production code requires a structured, collaborative workflow:
✔ Step 1 — Requirements & Understanding
I start with a session with the data scientist to understand:
Model purpose, assumptions, feature logic
Data dependencies (tables, files, APIs)
Hyperparameters, preprocessing steps
I capture all these in a design document or ML pipeline spec.

✔ Step 2 — Refactor Notebook into Modular Code
Break the notebook into reusable Python modules:
data_ingestion.py
feature_engineering.py
train.py
predict.py
evaluation.py
Convert inline/duplicate code cells into functions/classes.
Remove stateful notebook-specific artifacts.

✔ Step 3 — Add Standards
Pydantic for input validation
Logging (structured JSON logs)
Exception handling
Type hints + docstrings
Config separation (YAML/JSON + Hydra)

✔ Step 4 — Build Pipeline (Airflow / ADF / Prefect / MLflow)
Orchestrate:
data fetch
transform
training
validation
deployment

✔ Step 5 — Testing
Add:
unit tests (pytest)
integration tests (train → predict flow)
data quality checks (Great Expectations)

✔ Step 6 — Containerization & CI/CD
Dockerize model code
Add CI/CD for:
linting
tests
model registry push
automatic deployment

✔ Step 7 — Monitoring
Set up model performance monitoring:
input drift
prediction drift
latency
infra metrics

⭐ 2. When reviewing code from data scientists, what are the top 5 things you look for?
Answer:

1. Modularity & Maintainability
Code broken into functions/classes—not long notebook cells.
Separation of concerns (ingestion, preprocessing, training, inference).

2. Performance & Scalability
Vectorized operations (pandas → Spark/Polars if needed)
Memory usage
No local file paths or hard-coded values

3. Reproducibility
Random seeds
Environment captured (requirements.txt, conda.yaml)
Deterministic data transforms

4. Error Handling & Logging
Try/except blocks for critical parts
Structured logs instead of print()

5. Configurability
Parameters passed via config files
No hard-coded credentials/API keys
Environment variables used properly

⭐ 3. How do you translate business requirements into technical ML/GenAI solutions?
Answer:

Step 1 — Understand the Business Problem
I ask:
What business metric are we trying to improve?
What decisions will the model influence?
Expected ROI?

Step 2 — Convert to ML Problem Type
Examples:
“Reduce manual customer support work” → GenAI summarization/chatbot
“Predict demand for products” → forecasting model

Step 3 — Identify Data Requirements
Source systems (Snowflake, Azure SQL, APIs)
Required features
Data quality & availability

Step 4 — Architecture Mapping
I define:
data preprocessing pipeline
model training approach
deployment method (batch vs real-time)
infra (AKS / ACI / serverless / MLflow)

Step 5 — Constraints
Latency requirements
Security/compliance (PII, PHI)
Cost limits

Step 6 — Produce High-Level Design
Includes:
Data flow diagram
Model lifecycle diagram
Error paths
Monitoring strategy

Step 7 — Validate with Stakeholders
Align with Product Owner, DS, and Infra team
Convert into user stories + tasks

⭐ 4. Describe a situation where you challenged a design decision and improved the system.
Answer example:

"In one project, data scientists wanted to deploy a heavy XGBoost model inside a Flask API on a VM. The latency target was < 50 ms, but the model took 300+ ms just to load. I challenged the design because it wouldn't scale or meet SLA.
I proposed moving to an ONNX optimized version of the model and running it on AKS with autoscaling and Azu­re ML Managed Endpoints. I also implemented caching for common requests.
Outcome:
Inference latency dropped from 300 ms → 20 ms
Cost reduced by 40% due to auto-scaling
Reliability improved because probes + auto-healing handled failures
This example shows I challenge decisions based on technical constraints and propose a scalable alternative."

⭐ 5. How do you balance speed of delivery vs reliability in ML deployments?
Answer:

This is a common tradeoff in MLOps — I follow a framework:
✔ 1. Development Speed for New Experiments
Allow flexible notebook experimentation
Enable ephemeral environments
Provide shared feature store + datasets

✔ 2. Reliability for Production
To avoid slowing down delivery, I enforce automation:
Minimal reliability standards:
CI pipeline with linting + unit tests
MLflow model registry for approval
Canary or shadow deployments
Automated validation (data drift, schema checks)

✔ 3. Controlled Release Strategy
Use A/B testing, blue/green, or shadow mode to reduce risk.
Feature flags to switch models instantly.

✔ 4. Clear Escape Plan
Automated rollback if:
model accuracy drops
latency > threshold
drift detected

✔ 5. Communication with Stakeholders
Agree on:
acceptable risk
deployment schedule
fallback scenarios
In short:
Move fast in development, deploy safely using automation and guardrails.